It was inscribed by John H. in the annals dated 1/12/05 9:41 AM:
[adding back in snipped attribution] Kevin Wayne wrote:

>>You contradict yourself here. If you're going to say "There are
>>plenty of reasons to play Nethack," then it doesn't make sense
 >>to turn around and say, "Going for a high score is basically the
>>way to play." What if someone's reason for playing Nethack has 
 >>nothing to do with score?
> 
> I meant "the way to play" in a different sense.  New players, who don't
> know about how to win, can at least use high score as an objective, if
> they need one, to inspire further play, a measure of improvement they
> can see go up. 

The relevant point here is, "if they need one." I think the game itself 
gives plenty of feedback on how well you did. Most of us in our 
pre-ascension days probably rate it by how far down in the dungeon we got.

The reason I wrote what I did is because any time any of us suggest 
something that would make the game harder, or penalize types of play we 
feel are exploitative of the game, a Certain Poster screams that we are 
fascists trying to ruin other people's fun. His point is that people 
play for a variety of reasons, not all related to ascension, and 
therefore the game shouldn't eliminate or penalize types of play that 
are Different.

Fine, says I, we won't make assumptions about why people play. But then 
let's really make *no* assumptions. And at that point, any scoring 
system becomes meaningless, because there is no way to know how well the 
criteria of score match the reasons that people play.

 > Ascension seems hopelessly out of reach until you do
> it; in my opinion, that's part of what gives Nethack its charm, when
> you win, you can impress any arbitrary non-winning player.  But until
> you see how winning can be possible, even not that hard, it seems like
> a great wall to climb.

And an unspoiled player will not even know that "ascension" exists, or 
perhaps that the game can be won (the manpage suggests that it can't). 
The lure of the game, at that stage, is to see how far you can go.

None of this really requires score at all. The only real reason for 
score at all is to be able to build a high-score table which will rank 
games. As you say in your original post, the way a typical player will 
play will cause a game that "feels" better to have a higher score. And 
that's probably the best we can expect from a scoring system. The point 
at which the system breaks down is for advanced players, who can create 
an arbitrarily high score with little risk by arbitrarily prolonging the 
midgame (regardless of whether they ascend or not).
